Pelvic inflammatory disease
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Cervical motion tenderness, Bilateral adnexal tenderness, Purulent vaginal discharge

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe bilateral lower abdominal pain, high fever, and foul-smelling discharge requiring immediate assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated rapid onset of severe, bilateral pelvic pain, a high fever (39.0 C), and purulent vaginal discharge. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to immediately diagnose acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID), rule out a tubo-ovarian abscess or acute appendicitis, and initiate broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy to prevent permanent tubal damage or systemic sepsis before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My lower stomach is throbbing on both sides, I am burning up with a fever, and I have this horrible, thick, smelly discharge.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated rapidly over the past 48 hours. The patient reports severe, bilateral, dull and aching lower abdominal and pelvic pain that has now become constant. This is accompanied by a new, abnormal, mucopurulent, foul-smelling vaginal discharge. She has been experiencing a high fever, recorded at 39.1 C at home, along with chills. She reports severe deep dyspareunia during sexual intercourse over the weekend, followed by irregular intermenstrual spotting. She also notes mild dysuria and urinary frequency. She denies right upper quadrant pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound; walking or sudden movements severely exacerbate the pelvic pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of progression to tubo-ovarian abscess or sepsis if untreated.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 39.2 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears systemically unwell, flushed, diaphoretic, and in significant pain, walking with a stooped posture.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals marked bilateral lower quadrant tenderness, with mild guarding but no generalized rigid peritonism. Bowel sounds are normal. No right upper quadrant tenderness (negative Murphy's sign, no obvious Fitz-Hugh-Curtis). Speculum examination reveals copious, thick, yellow mucopurulent cervical discharge. Bimanual pelvic examination is exquisitely painful: there is pronounced cervical motion tenderness (positive chandelier sign) and exquisite, bilateral adnexal and uterine tenderness to palpation. No distinct, fluctuant adnexal mass (tubo-ovarian abscess) is palpable, though guarding makes deep palpation difficult.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment; water bottle by the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol 1g four hours ago with minimal effect on the fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) - Status: Severe, febrile
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out by the distinctly bilateral nature of the pain, the presence of purulent cervical discharge, and pronounced cervical motion tenderness.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out by a negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test.

Urinary Tract Infection / Pyelonephritis - Unlikely to be the primary cause given the severe, bilateral adnexal tenderness and purulent cervical discharge, though dysuria is a shared symptom.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Medical risk is high for tubal damage)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark diagnostic tetrad of acute Pelvic Inflammatory Disease: high fever, bilateral adnexal/uterine tenderness, pronounced cervical motion tenderness, and purulent vaginal discharge. The objective clinical evidence strongly confirms an acute, ascending pelvic infection. The presence of a high fever and tachycardia indicates a significant systemic inflammatory response. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to immediately administer high-dose parenteral antibiotics (ceftriaxone) and prescribe a robust oral regimen. Delaying this treatment until the next in-hours period would have drastically increased the risk of irreversible fallopian tubal scarring, infertility, or the formation of a life-threatening tubo-ovarian abscess.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular Ceftriaxone 500mg immediately (first-line therapy for gonococcal coverage). Prescribed oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily for 14 days and oral Metronidazole 400mg twice daily for 14 days. Collected high vaginal and endocervical swabs for nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) for Chlamydia and Gonorrhea, to be dropped at the lab tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To ensure the infection is completely eradicated, you must complete the entire 14-day course of both oral antibiotics even if you feel completely better in two days; understanding that this prevents permanent infertility will drive strict adherence. To prevent reinfection, abstain from any sexual intercourse until both you and any partners have completely finished treatment; observing the resolution of all symptoms will validate this necessary resting period.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 48-72 hours to ensure clinical improvement (fever reduction, pain improvement) and review swab results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if your fever does not begin to drop within 24 hours, if you develop intractable vomiting and cannot keep the antibiotics down, or if the pain suddenly becomes sharp, severe, and unbearable.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a serious infection that requires strict adherence to a long course of antibiotics to prevent long-term complications like infertility.
Clinical Course: The injection tonight will rapidly target the most aggressive bacteria. The fever should break and the pain should start to improve significantly within 48 hours of starting the oral antibiotics.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you cannot keep the oral antibiotics down due to vomiting, or if the pain becomes rapidly worse.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have a severe bacterial infection that has travelled from your vagina up into your uterus and fallopian tubes. The injection provided tonight is a powerful antibiotic to stop the immediate spread. The 14-day course of tablets is absolutely necessary to kill every remaining bacteria hidden in the tissues and prevent permanent scarring that could stop you from having children in the future.
Actionable Steps: Start the oral doxycycline and metronidazole immediately tonight. Strictly avoid all alcohol while taking metronidazole to prevent severe vomiting. You must see your GP in 48 hours for a check-up.

